## 1. SQL Schema (CREATE TABLE)

The `memo` table is defined in the `LATEST.sql` migration file for each of the three supported databases. All three share the same logical columns:

- **SQLite**: `/bench/home/work/store/migration/sqlite/LATEST.sql` -- line 33
- **PostgreSQL**: `/bench/home/work/store/migration/postgres/LATEST.sql` -- line 32
- **MySQL**: `/bench/home/work/store/migration/mysql/LATEST.sql` -- line 32

The table columns are: `id`, `uid`, `creator_id`, `created_ts`, `updated_ts`, `row_status`, `content`, `visibility`, `pinned`, `payload`. There are also related tables `memo_relation` and `memo_share` defined later in those same files.

---

## 2. Go Store Model (struct)

The Go store-layer struct is defined in:

- **`/bench/home/work/store/memo.go`** -- line 35

```go
type Memo struct {
    ID         int32
    UID        string
    RowStatus  RowStatus
    CreatorID  int32
    CreatedTs  int64
    UpdatedTs  int64
    Content    string
    Visibility Visibility
    Pinned     bool
    Payload    *storepb.MemoPayload
    ParentUID  *string
}
```

This struct maps directly to the SQL table columns. The `Payload` field references a protobuf type `MemoPayload` defined in the store-layer proto file `/bench/home/work/proto/store/memo.proto` (line 7).

---

## 3. API Protobuf Message

The API-layer protobuf message is defined in:

- **`/bench/home/work/proto/api/v1/memo_service.proto`** -- line 198

```protobuf
message Memo {
  string name = 1;
  State state = 2;
  string creator = 3;
  google.protobuf.Timestamp create_time = 4;
  google.protobuf.Timestamp update_time = 5;
  string content = 7;
  Visibility visibility = 9;
  repeated string tags = 10;
  bool pinned = 11;
  repeated Attachment attachments = 12;
  repeated MemoRelation relations = 13;
  repeated Reaction reactions = 14;
  Property property = 15;
  optional string parent = 16;
  string snippet = 17;
  optional Location location = 18;
  // ... nested message Property { ... }
}
```

This is the API-facing representation, distinct from the store model -- it uses resource names (`memos/{memo}`) rather than integer IDs, timestamps instead of unix epoch integers, and includes computed/output-only fields like `tags`, `reactions`, `property`, `snippet`, and `parent`.

There is also a separate store-layer proto file at `/bench/home/work/proto/store/memo.proto` (line 7), but it defines `message MemoPayload` (the JSON payload column), not the `Memo` message itself.

---

